\description{
Aggregates an sf point dataset into one row per cell. By default computes
counts and means, but the aggregation function is configurable. It can
also return a pooled value for each cell, its mean pulled toward the mean
of the cells around it, and a range for one further observation.
}
\details{
This is the third step of the package's pipeline, taking the labelled layer
from \code{\link[=assign_features_to_polygons]{assign_features_to_polygons()}} down to cell level. What distinguishes
it from a plain \code{dplyr::group_by()} + \code{summarise()} is that it carries the
\emph{uncertainty} of each aggregate with it: alongside every mean it returns a
within-cell standard deviation, a standard error and an observation count,
and it can correct that standard error for within-cell spatial
autocorrelation via \code{deff}. Reach for it whenever the cell-level values will
be modelled or mapped, because a cell mean over 2 observations and one over
200 are not the same measurement and nothing downstream can tell them apart
otherwise.

In addition to user-specified aggregation functions, this function always
computes within-cell standard deviation (\verb{..sd_<var>}) and standard error
(\verb{..se_<var>}) for every numeric response/predictor column, plus an \code{n}
column (rows falling in the cell) and a \code{cell_weight} column.
These columns let downstream models account for the fact that a cell with
2 observations carries more aggregation uncertainty than one with 200.

\code{cell_weight} is the \emph{effective} sample size of the primary variable: the
response when one was supplied, otherwise the first predictor. It counts
that variable's non-missing rows, not all rows (a cell of 10 rows with 3
finite responses carries 3 observations' worth of information about the
response, not 10), and it is divided by that cell's design effect when
\code{deff} applied one. With \code{deff = 1} and no missing values it equals \code{n}.
Pass it as the \code{weights} argument of a downstream regression.

An infinite value (\code{Inf}, \code{-Inf}) in a response or predictor column is
treated as missing, as \code{NA} and \code{NaN} are, in every statistic of that
column: the \code{agg_funs} results, \verb{..sd_*}, \verb{..se_*}, the interval columns,
\verb{..neff_*}, \code{cell_weight} and the design effect. \code{n} still counts the row.
A warning of class \code{"spatialkit_nonfinite"} names the columns and gives the
number of rows and cells affected (the condition carries \code{n} and
\code{columns}). \code{\link[=kriging_adequacy]{kriging_adequacy()}}, \code{\link[=resolution_profile]{resolution_profile()}},
\code{\link[=estimate_sac_range]{estimate_sac_range()}} and \code{\link[=prep_model_data]{prep_model_data()}} leave such rows out too, so
the steps of one pipeline agree on a cell's mean. A function in \code{agg_funs}
therefore never sees an infinite value: \code{max} returns the largest finite
value, and infinities cannot be counted through \code{agg_funs}.
}
\section{Spatial autocorrelation and standard-error bias}{
By default (\code{deff = 1}), the \verb{..se_*} columns are computed as
\code{sd / sqrt(n)}, which treats the observations within each cell as
independent. When data are spatially autocorrelated (the common case for the
spatial workflows this package supports), within-cell observations are
typically positively correlated: they share the cell's departure from the
population mean, so as an estimate of the \strong{population (grand) mean} a cell
mean has an effective sample size smaller than \code{n}. For that estimand the
naive SE is \strong{anticonservative} (too small), and a downstream weighted
regression that uses \code{cell_weight} or the \verb{..se_*} columns for population-level
inference will produce overconfident standard errors for cells with strong
intra-cell correlation. For the cell's \strong{own} mean the naive SE is the right
one when the cell's points are spread through it, and the corrected SE is too
wide; see "What the standard error estimates" before setting \code{deff}.

Setting \code{deff = "kish"} applies an approximate correction using Kish's
design effect. An intra-class correlation (ICC) is estimated for every
summarised column on its own, the response and each predictor, via a
one-way random-effects decomposition across all cells. Each column's ICC
is used for that column's SE adjustment, and each cell's effective sample
size is reduced to \code{n_i / (1 + (n_i - 1) * rho)}. This is a first-order
correction that does not require a full spatial covariance model but does
require enough cells and observations for a stable ICC estimate.

A design effect estimated from the data (\code{"kish"} or \code{"variogram"}) comes
with a second, small-sample correction. The within-cell correlation that
inflates the variance of the mean to \code{sigma^2 * deff / n} also biases the
within-cell sample variance downward: under exchangeable correlation \code{rho}
(Kish's own assumption), with \code{deff = 1 + (n - 1) * rho},
\code{E[s^2] = sigma^2 * (n - deff) / (n - 1)}, so \code{s^2} understates \code{sigma^2}
by very nearly the factor by which \code{deff} inflates the mean's variance, and
the two errors compound rather than cancel. The standard error is therefore
\code{s * sqrt(deff / n) * sqrt((n - 1) / (n - deff))}, and \code{NA} where
\code{deff >= n} (the cell then holds one observation's worth of information
and \code{s} carries none about \code{sigma}). This is the package's own derivation,
not taken from a reference. Measured 95\% interval coverage at \code{n = 30}
over 20,000 replicates: 0.921, 0.844 and 0.628 at \code{rho} = 0.2, 0.5 and 0.8
with \code{s * sqrt(deff / n)} alone, against 0.948, 0.950 and 0.949 with the
rescaling.

You may also pass a fixed numeric design effect (e.g. \code{deff = 2}) to
uniformly inflate standard errors: an externally supplied constant is
applied as \code{sd * sqrt(deff / n)}, exactly \code{sqrt(deff)} times the naive SE in
every cell. (The \code{E[s^2]} correction that the estimated design effects also
apply is derived from within-cell correlation and would not be justified for
a number the caller chose.)

Even with the Kish correction, the adjusted SE is an approximation.
For rigorous inference under spatial dependence, consider fitting an
explicit spatial covariance model (e.g. via \code{\link{fit_bayesian_spatial_model}}).
}

\section{What the standard error estimates}{
What the \verb{..se_*} columns estimate follows \code{deff}. At the default,
\code{deff = 1}, \code{sd / sqrt(n)} is the standard error of \strong{the cell's own
mean}, the mean of the units the cell holds, and it is the right one when
the cell's observations are spread through the cell. Under
\code{deff = "kish"}, \code{"variogram"} or a number other than 1 the columns are the
standard error of the cell mean \strong{as an estimate of the population (grand)
mean}: the unconditional quantity, in which the cell's own realised
deviation is part of the error, widened for the correlation inside the
cell. That is the right quantity when cells are treated as samples from a
common population: measured 95\% interval coverage of the grand mean is
0.95 with \code{deff = "kish"} (and 0.29 with the naive SE) on exchangeable
within-cell correlation, and 0.93 with \code{deff = "variogram"} on a simulated
Gaussian field.

The corrected standard error is \strong{not} that of the cell's own mean (the
block average over that cell), which is what a cell-level map or a
regression on cell values usually wants. For that quantity the naive
\code{sd / sqrt(n)} is the
better of the two on offer: measured coverage 0.95 under exchangeable
within-cell correlation, against very nearly 1.00 for the
design-effect-corrected SE, which is too wide by the factor
\code{sqrt(deff / (1 - rho))} (4.6 at 20 points a cell and \code{rho = 0.5}). That 0.95
is exact under the exchangeable model and holds under a spatial covariance
model only when the cell's points are spread through the cell; with
\emph{clustered} sampling inside a cell it is anticonservative for the block
average too (measured 0.58), and the right quantity there is a
block-kriging variance, which this function does not compute. Use \code{deff}
when the cell means feed a population-level inference; leave it at 1 when
they are measurements of the cells themselves and the sampling within cells
is reasonably uniform.
}

\section{Design effects and variable types}{
\code{deff = "kish"} estimates a separate ICC for each summarised column, the
response and every predictor, and applies each to that column alone:
listing or removing a predictor leaves every other column's standard error,
effective n and interval as they were. (One ICC used to be pooled over all
the predictors, which was right for a column only when they all shared it:
beside a predictor with an ICC of 0.87, one with an ICC of 0 had its
standard error multiplied by 3.1.) \code{deff = "variogram"} fits or
accepts \strong{one} correlation function and applies it to every numeric column,
because a variogram is a property of the field being modelled rather than of
a variable type; a predictor whose spatial structure differs markedly from
the response's will have its SE corrected by the response's correlation.
The internally estimated variogram is fitted to the \strong{response itself},
never to OLS residuals, whatever \code{predictor_vars} holds: the \verb{..se_resp_*}
columns estimate the grand mean of the response, so the correlation to
correct for is the response's own. (A residual variogram, whose correlation
is that of the part the predictors do not explain, is weaker; using it here
dropped grand-mean coverage from 0.93 to 0.51 the moment a predictor was
listed.) Pass \code{sac} explicitly when you want a different variogram, such as
a residual one from \code{estimate_sac_range(..., predictor_vars = )}. A \code{sac}
whose \code{attr(sac, "detrended")} is \code{TRUE} is used as given, but when it
corrects response columns a warning says that their standard errors are
understated, as \code{\link[=kriging_adequacy]{kriging_adequacy()}} warns about the same mismatch.
}

\section{Confidence intervals}{
With \code{conf_level} set, every numeric response and predictor column gains
four more columns: \verb{..neff_*}, that column's effective sample size in the
cell (its non-missing count over its design effect, the per-column version
of \code{cell_weight}); \verb{..df_*}, the degrees of freedom the interval uses; and
\verb{..ci_lo_*} / \verb{..ci_hi_*}, a t interval for a mean. Which mean follows
\code{deff}, as for \verb{..se_*}: at the default, \code{deff = 1}, \strong{the cell's own
mean}, when its observations are spread through the cell; under
\code{deff = "kish"}, \code{"variogram"} or a number other than 1, the \strong{cell mean as
an estimate of the population (grand) mean}. Everything in "What the
standard error estimates" applies to it, including that the corrected
interval is not one for the cell's own block average. The interval is
\verb{mean +/- qt((1 + conf_level) / 2, df) * se}, centred on the plain mean of
the column's non-missing values whatever \code{agg_funs} computes, and is \code{NA}
wherever the standard error is (a single observation; complete redundancy
under \code{deff}). \verb{..neff_*} and \verb{..df_*} are \code{NA} where the column has one
non-missing value or none, like the standard error; \code{cell_weight} still
counts such a cell (1, or \code{1 / deff} for a numeric \code{deff}), so the two
differ there.

At no setting is \verb{..ci_*} a range for one observation: it is an interval
for a mean, and it narrows as a cell's count grows where the spread of
single observations does not. The interval for one further observation in
a cell has its own argument and columns, \code{obs_level} with
\code{estimate = "pooled"} and \verb{..obs_lo_*} / \verb{..obs_hi_*} (see "Intervals:
which one answers which question"). In the simulation described there,
\verb{..ci_*} at \code{conf_level = 0.90} held 58.9 percent of the observations held
out among the others, and the interval for one observation at
\code{obs_level = 0.90} held 89.6 percent. Away from the observations no
interval here is calibrated.

The degrees of freedom are \strong{not} \code{neff - 1}. The interval's spread comes
from the within-cell sample variance, and under exchangeable correlation
(\code{deff = "kish"}) that variance keeps its \code{n - 1} degrees of freedom
whatever the design effect. The design effect inflates the mean's
variance and biases \code{s^2}, both of which the standard error already
corrects, and the resulting pivot is exactly t on \code{n - 1} df. Measured 95\%
coverage of the grand mean on the Kish path, 20 cells of 20 at an ICC of
0.2 / 0.6 / 0.9: 0.954 / 0.953 / 0.952 with \code{n - 1}, against
0.992 / 1.000 / 1.000 with \code{neff - 1}, which is not an interval so much as
a refusal to say anything. The effective-sample-size degrees of freedom of
Faes et al. (2009) belong to a mean estimated across many correlated units
whose variance is estimated from the spread between them; they do not
transfer to a single cell's mean with a variance estimated from inside it.
\verb{..df_*} is therefore \code{n - 1} at \code{deff = 1}, for a numeric \code{deff} and for
\code{"kish"}. Under \code{deff = "variogram"} correlation decays with distance and
the within-cell variance loses degrees of freedom to it; \verb{..df_*} is then
the Satterthwaite (1946) moment-matched df of \code{s^2} under the fitted
correlation matrix, a fraction of \code{n - 1} that shrinks as the range grows.
Measured on a Gaussian field with an exponential range of 150 and 400 on a
1000-unit domain, 16 cells of about 25 points: 0.960 and 0.958 with that
df, against 0.931 and 0.918 with \code{n - 1}, and 0.34 and 0.19 for the naive
\code{deff = 1} interval. The interval is only as good as the design effect
under it: a mis-specified variogram, or an ICC estimated from too few
cells, moves the coverage with it.
}

\section{Pooled values}{
With \code{estimate = "pooled"} every numeric column also gets a pooled value.
A cell's value, plain or pooled, estimates the cell's \emph{level}: the
expected value of one further observation located in the cell the way its
observations were. It is not the average of a surface over the cell's
area, which would give weight to ground that holds no observation.

For one column, write \code{n}, \code{S} and \code{m = S / n} for the count, the sum and
the mean of a cell's rows, \code{N} for the rows in all and \code{K} for the cells
that hold one. Then
\itemize{
\item \code{sigma2 = sum((y - m)^2) / (N - K)} is the spread of the observed values
about their cell's mean;
\item the target \code{t} of a cell is the mean of the rows of its neighbours,
\code{sum(S) / sum(n)} over them. The neighbours are the 8 cells whose
centroids are nearest, the centroid of a cell being the mean of the
coordinates of its rows. The polygons are not read, so two cells are
neighbours whether or not they touch, and a cell is never its own
neighbour;
\item \code{tau2 = max(0, (sum(n * (m - t)^2) - K * sigma2) / N)} is the mean
squared distance between a cell's level and its target, the target's own
error included;
\item the own weight is \code{w = tau2 / (tau2 + sigma2 / n)};
\item the pooled value is \code{w * m + (1 - w) * t}.
}

The own weight is the share of the pooled value that is the cell's own
mean. It grows with the cell's count and with \code{tau2}. At 1 the pooled
value is the plain mean; at 0 it is the target and holds none of the
cell's own values. Pooled values are for maps and for predicting
observations. \verb{..pooled_w_*} is not a regression weight. The plain
columns are the same under either \code{estimate}, and they are the ones to
use, with \code{cell_weight}, in a regression on cell values: each column is
pulled toward its neighbours by its own amount, so a regression of pooled
responses on pooled predictors is not a regression of the levels.

The cells can be any polygons the rows were assigned to, the package's
own or ones the caller brings, with IDs of any type: only each row's cell
and location are read. Each column is pooled on its own rows, those with
a value, a cell and a location, with its own centroids, neighbours,
\code{sigma2} and \code{tau2}; a
missing value in one column changes nothing in another. A cell with one
such row gets a value. A cell with none gets \code{NA}, never a value made from
its neighbours. The pooled columns do not read \code{agg_funs} or \code{deff}: the
mean that is pooled is the arithmetic mean whatever \code{agg_funs} computes,
and no design effect enters \code{sigma2}, \code{tau2}, the weights or the interval
of \code{obs_level}.

Three cases change what the numbers rest on, and each is a warning (see
"Conditions"). With 2 to 9 cells the neighbours of a cell are all the
others, so its target is the mean of every row outside it, and the overall
mean lies between that target and the cell's own mean. A cell whose own
weight is below its share of the rows, \code{n / N}, then gets a pooled value
on the far side of the overall mean from its plain mean, and two cells can
change order. When few cells hold two rows, \code{sigma2} is estimated from few
values and every weight rests on it: \code{df} in \code{attr(, "pooled")} is the
number of values beyond one per cell. And when \code{tau2} comes out at 0,
every own weight is 0 and each pooled value is its target.

A tie between two candidate neighbours goes to the centroid with the
lower x, then the lower y, and the centroids are summed with the rows in
coordinate order, so a cell's neighbours do not depend on the order of the
rows. The values then agree to rounding: a cell's sums are taken in the
order of its rows.
The tie is between distances computed from the coordinates in the CRS the
distances are measured in (see \code{estimate}), so on gridded locations the
same layer in another CRS or unit can give a cell another neighbour.

The figures on this page that describe pooled values and the two
intervals come from one simulation, \code{dev/sim-pooled-values.R} in the
package's repository, whose header holds its settings and seeds. It draws 200
layers on a square of side 1,000. Each has 2,100 clustered locations
(four fifths of them round 25 parent points with a standard deviation of
40, the rest uniform) and a response that is a field with covariance
\code{exp(-d / 100)} plus noise of variance 0.3. The 300 locations nearest a
random centre are held out as a block, 300 of the others are held out at
random, and the 1,500 left are the layer: 333 cells at 4.5 observations a
cell from \code{\link[=resolution_profile]{resolution_profile()}} with \code{partition = "density"},
\code{select_resolution(criterion = "size")}, \code{\link[=get_voronoi_seeds]{get_voronoi_seeds()}},
\code{\link[=build_tessellation]{build_tessellation()}} and \code{\link[=assign_features_to_polygons]{assign_features_to_polygons()}}, summarised
with \code{estimate = "pooled"}. A held-out observation is given the value of
its cell by \code{\link[=cell_values_at]{cell_values_at()}}. At the observations held out at random
the root mean squared error of the pooled value was 0.96 of the plain
mean's: 0.84 in cells that hold one of the layer's observations, 0.95 in
cells of 2 to 4, 0.98 in cells of 5 to 9 and 0.99 in cells of 10 or
more. In the held-out block it was 0.95.

\code{structured} in \code{attr(, "pooled")} says whether these cells predict a
left-out observation better than one mean for all rows. It is \code{FALSE}
when the leave-one-out error of one mean (\code{loo_one_cell}) is within 2
percent of that of the pooled values (\code{loo}) or below it, or when the F
test of the cell means against one mean has \code{p >= 0.05}
(\code{structure_p}). \code{FALSE} raises a warning of class
\code{"spatialkit_no_structure"}, and the values are returned. On a response
without spatial structure they still make a smooth map, because each is
mostly the mean of its neighbours. With white noise as the response on
the simulation's 200 layers the median own weight was 0.11, \code{tau2} was 0
on 20 layers, the warning was raised on all 200, and a cell's pooled
value had a correlation of 0.79 with the mean of the pooled values of its
8 nearest cells, where the plain means had one of -0.02.

The pooling model implies an error variance for a pooled value as an
estimate of the cell's level, \code{(1 - w) * tau2}. It is not returned, and
neither is an interval for the level, because no check has confirmed an
interval built from it; \code{sigma2}, \code{tau2} and \verb{..pooled_w_*} are in the
result for a reader who wants to form it. In the simulation, with a
cell's level taken as the mean of the noise-free field over further
locations drawn in it like the data's, the squared error of the pooled
value was 0.98 of \code{(1 - w) * tau2} among the observations and 6.9 times
it in the held-out block: away from the observations it is too small.
}

\section{Intervals: which one answers which question}{
Two families of interval columns can be in the frame. Each has its own
argument, and neither changes the other.\tabular{lllll}{
   Family \tab Columns \tab Target \tab Centre \tab Asked for by \cr
   observation \tab \verb{..obs_lo_*}, \verb{..obs_hi_*} \tab one further observation in the cell \tab pooled value \tab \code{obs_level} \cr
   mean \tab \verb{..ci_lo_*}, \verb{..ci_hi_*} \tab a mean, never one observation \tab plain mean \tab \code{conf_level} \cr
}


The observation of the first is one located in the cell the way the
data's were. \code{obs_level} needs \code{estimate = "pooled"}: without it the call
is an error, not an interval round the plain mean. The mean the second
covers follows \code{deff}: at \code{deff = 1} the cell's own mean, when its
observations are spread through the cell; under \code{deff = "kish"},
\code{"variogram"} or a number other than 1 the cell mean as an estimate of
the population mean (see "Confidence intervals", which also describes
\verb{..neff_*} and \verb{..df_*}). No interval and no standard error is returned
for a cell's level as the pooled value estimates it.
\code{attr(, "intervals")} says the same of the frame in hand, one row per
family present.

The interval for one observation is the pooled value plus two quantiles
of the leave-one-out residuals of the pooled values. The residual \code{e} of
a row is its value minus the pooled value of its cell computed without
it: the cell's mean and own weight are computed again without the row,
and \code{sigma2}, \code{tau2} and the targets are held. With
\code{p = (1 - obs_level) / 2},
\code{..obs_lo = pooled + quantile(e, p)} and
\code{..obs_hi = pooled + quantile(e, 1 - p)}.
\itemize{
\item It is a statement about observations on average: of further
observations located like the data, about \code{obs_level} of them fall
inside the interval of their cell. It does not hold cell by cell.
\item The two offsets are the same in every cell. The interval's centre
varies over the map and its width does not. It does not narrow where a
cell holds more rows: the offsets are quantiles of the errors of
predicting single observations, and the spread of observations inside a
cell is in every one of them.
\item It is wide. One observation is uncertain.
\item Only the level 0.90 has been checked. Another level is computed the
same way.
\item Each residual is the error of predicting an observation from the other
rows of its cell and from the cells around it, so the interval
describes locations among the observations. A cell's value and interval
describe the part of the cell where its observations lie, and a Voronoi
cell can reach far past them toward the edge of the study area. The
interval is too narrow for a cell with no observation, a gap in the
layer or ground beyond the layer's edge.
}

In the simulation described under "Pooled values", the interval at
\code{obs_level = 0.90} held 89.6 percent of the observations held out at
random among the others (83.7 to 94.0 percent in single layers) and 75.1
percent of those in the held-out block. \verb{..ci_*} at \code{conf_level = 0.90},
read as a range for one observation, held 58.9 and 49.2 percent of them,
over the held-out observations whose cell has that interval.
\code{\link[=cell_values_at]{cell_values_at()}} returns each location's distance from the nearest
observation, and its help gives the coverage by that distance.

Away from the observations no interval here is calibrated.
}

\section{Conditions}{
What the pooled columns and the count record have to say is raised as R
warnings with a class, so \verb{tryCatch(<class> = )} and
\code{withCallingHandlers()} catch each without matching its text. A warning
of the pooled columns is raised once per call and reason, for all the
columns it applies to: its text names them and the condition carries them
in \code{columns}, in the order of the pooled columns. A field said to be by
column is a vector named by those columns, and the numbers of each column
are in \code{attr(, "pooled")}.
\itemize{
\item \code{"spatialkit_pool_unavailable"}: the pooled columns of a column are
\code{NA}. The plain columns are returned, and this is never an error.
\code{reason} is \code{"no coordinates"} (a layer without geometry),
\code{"no located value"} (no row with a value, a cell and a location),
\code{"no cell holds two values"} (the condition also carries \code{n} and
\code{cells} by column) or \code{"work limit"}. The last is the case of many
cells without the 'FNN' package: the neighbour search then compares
every pair of cells and is not made above
\code{getOption("spatialkit.exact_pairs_max", 2e9)} pairs, which is about
44,700 cells (the condition also carries \code{work}, \code{limit}, and \code{cells}
by column). With 'FNN' there is no such limit, and the result is the
same.
\item \code{"spatialkit_pool_degenerate"}: the pooled values were computed and
carry no pooling. \code{reason} is \code{"one cell"} (the pooled value is the
plain mean; also \code{n} by column), \code{"no spread inside any cell"} (every
own weight is 1; also \code{constant} by column) or \code{"no signal between cells"} (\code{tau2} is 0 and every own weight is 0; also \code{sigma2} by
column).
\item \code{"spatialkit_pool_few_cells"}: 2 to 9 cells, so each target is the mean
of all the other cells (\code{cells}, and \code{own_weight}, the smallest, by
column).
\item \code{"spatialkit_pool_few_df"}: \code{sigma2} rests on fewer than 30 values
beyond one per cell (\code{needed}, and \code{df}, \code{n} and \code{cells} by column).
\item \code{"spatialkit_no_structure"}: \code{structured} is \code{FALSE} (see "Pooled
values"). The text says which of the two tests failed, and \code{beats} is
\code{TRUE} for a column whose pooled values beat one mean while the F test
does not tell the cell means from one mean (also \code{criterion = "loo"},
\code{estimate = "pooled"}, and by column \code{n}, the cells, \code{one_cell}, \code{loo}
and \code{structure_p}).
\item \code{"spatialkit_pool_rows_skipped"}: rows with a value and a cell have
empty or non-finite coordinates. They count in \code{n} and the plain
columns and take no part in the pooled ones (\code{n}, \code{cells}).
\item \code{"spatialkit_repeated_locations"}, with \code{obs_level}: rows with a value
share a location. A residual leaves one row out and keeps the others at
its location, so the interval then describes a further observation at a
place already observed (\code{n} and \code{total} by column).
\item \code{"spatialkit_few_residuals"}, with \code{obs_level}: fewer than five
residuals are expected beyond each end of the interval, which at 0.90
is fewer than 100 residuals (\code{needed}, \code{level}, and \code{n} by column).
\item \code{"spatialkit_estimate_mismatch"} and \code{"spatialkit_cells_differ"}: the
two checks of the count record, described under "Value".
}

The two about few cells and few values are raised for columns whose
weights were estimated, and the two of \code{obs_level} for columns whose
interval is read from residuals, so a constant column raises
\code{"spatialkit_pool_degenerate"} and no other.
}

\examples{
library(sf)
set.seed(1)
n <- 200
east  <- runif(n, 0, 100)
north <- runif(n, 0, 100)
# A response with spatial structure, so the within-cell ICC is not zero.
pts <- st_as_sf(
  data.frame(x = 5e5 + east, y = 5e6 + north,
             val = 5 + 0.02 * east + 0.02 * north + rnorm(n, sd = 0.5)),
  coords = c("x", "y"), crs = 32632
)
bnd <- st_sf(geometry = st_sfc(st_polygon(list(rbind(
  c(5e5, 5e6), c(5e5 + 100, 5e6), c(5e5 + 100, 5e6 + 100),
  c(5e5, 5e6 + 100), c(5e5, 5e6)
))), crs = 32632))
grid <- create_grid_polygons(bnd, target_cells = 9, type = "square")
assigned <- assign_features_to_polygons(pts, grid)

# IID standard errors (default) vs Kish design-effect adjustment.  The
# correction is large here, and meant to be: about 22 points per cell that
# all share the cell's part of the trend carry far fewer than 22
# independent pieces of information about the cell mean.
naive <- summarize_by_cell(assigned, response_var = "val")
kish  <- summarize_by_cell(assigned, response_var = "val", deff = "kish")
data.frame(n = naive$n,
           se_naive = naive$..se_resp_val,
           se_kish  = kish$..se_resp_val)
attr(kish, "deff_applied")   # method, icc_resp, icc_pred, per-cell deff

# A 95\% interval for each cell mean as an estimate of the grand mean, on
# the Kish-corrected standard error and n - 1 degrees of freedom.
ci <- summarize_by_cell(assigned, response_var = "val", deff = "kish",
                        conf_level = 0.95)
ci[, c("poly_id", "n", "resp_mean_val", "..neff_resp_val", "..df_resp_val",
       "..ci_lo_resp_val", "..ci_hi_resp_val")]

# Pooled values, and a range for one observation: a smooth field plus
# noise at 400 points, on an 8 x 8 grid, so that a cell holds about six.
set.seed(2)
east  <- runif(400, 0, 8000)
north <- runif(400, 0, 8000)
pts2 <- st_as_sf(
  data.frame(x = 5e5 + east, y = 5e6 + north,
             val = 10 + 3 * sin(east / 1500) + 3 * cos(north / 1200) +
               rnorm(400)),
  coords = c("x", "y"), crs = 32632
)
bnd2 <- st_sf(geometry = st_sfc(st_polygon(list(rbind(
  c(5e5, 5e6), c(5e5 + 8000, 5e6), c(5e5 + 8000, 5e6 + 8000),
  c(5e5, 5e6 + 8000), c(5e5, 5e6)
))), crs = 32632))
grid2 <- create_grid_polygons(bnd2, target_cells = 64, type = "square")
assigned2 <- assign_features_to_polygons(pts2, grid2)
pooled <- summarize_by_cell(assigned2, response_var = "val",
                            estimate = "pooled", obs_level = 0.90)
# The plain mean, the pooled value and the weight on the cell's own mean.
head(pooled[, c("poly_id", "n", "resp_mean_val", "..pooled_resp_val",
                "..pooled_w_resp_val")])
# The range for one further observation in a cell.
head(pooled[, c("poly_id", "..obs_lo_resp_val", "..obs_hi_resp_val")])
attr(pooled, "pooled")   # what was fitted, and what the interval covers
}
